    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nSuppose that we have a cube whose edges are [MATH: 2\\,\\textrm{cm}] in length, like the one shown below.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/d04832ca9d8be3328d8c1b27f8076fa5]\n\nContent:\nThe formula for the volume [MATH: V] of a cube with sides of length [MATH: s] is given by\n\nContent:\n[MATH: V = s^3.]\n\nContent:\nTo find the volume of this cube, we substitute [MATH: s=2 \\: \\textrm{cm}] into the formula. This gives\n\nContent:\n[MATH: V = (2\\, \\textrm{cm})^3 = 8 \\text{ cm}^3 .]\n\nContent:\nSo the volume of the cube is [MATH: 8\\,\\textrm{cm}^3.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Volume of a Cube\n\nExample Prompt:\n[IMG: /graphics/1dd283e56ed97a6cd063f6eefa842991]\n\nWhat is the volume of the cube?\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nThe volume [MATH: V] of a cube can be found using the formula\n\n[MATH: V = s^3,]\n\nwhere [MATH: s] is the side length of the cube.\n\nSubstituting [MATH: s=4\\,\\textrm{cm}] into the formula, we get\n[MATH: V = (4\\, \\textrm{cm})^3 = 64\\, \\textrm{cm}^3 .]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\nFind the volume of the cube whose sides each have length [MATH: 4in.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4000in^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 400in^{3}]\nc. [MATH: 64in^{3}]\nd. [MATH: 16in^{3}]\ne. [MATH: 24in^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-81476]\n\nPrompt:\nWhat is the volume of the cube?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 18cm^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 31cm^{3}]\nc. [MATH: 54cm^{3}]\nd. [MATH: 9cm^{3}]\ne. [MATH: 27cm^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Finding a Side Length of a Cube Given Its Volume\n\nExample Prompt:\nThe volume of a cube is [MATH: 729\\, \\textrm{cm}^3.] Find the length of the edges of the cube.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nSince the volume of a cube is\n\n[MATH: V = s^3,]\n\nwhere [MATH: s] is the length of an edge, we can find [MATH: s] as follows:\n\n[MATH: s = \\sqrt[3]{V} = \\sqrt[3]{729} = 9]\n\nTherefore, the length of the edges is [MATH: 9\\,\\textrm{cm}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\nThe volume of a cube is [MATH: 125cm^{3}.] Find the length of the edges of the cube.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3cm]\nb. [MATH: 4cm]\nc. [MATH: 6cm]\nd. [MATH: 5cm]\ne. [MATH: 10cm]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nQuestion Graphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/q-107178]\n\nPrompt:\nThe volume of the cube above is [MATH: 27cm^{3}.] What is the length of its sides?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 81cm]\nb. [MATH: 4cm]\nc. [MATH: 6cm]\nd. [MATH: 3cm]\ne. [MATH: 9cm]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: The Volume of a Cube: Word Problems\n\nExample Prompt:\nSix identical plastic cubic containers have a combined volume of [MATH: 48 \\: \\textrm{ft}^3.] Find the side length of a single container.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we find the volume of a single container:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{48 \\: \\textrm{ft}^3}{6} = 8 \\: \\textrm{ft}^3]\n\nNow, we need to find the side length of one container.\n\nSince the volume of a cube is\n\n[MATH: V = s^3,]\n\nwhere [MATH: s] is the length of a side, we can find [MATH: s] as follows:\n\n[MATH: s = \\sqrt[3]{V} = \\sqrt[3]{8} = 2]\n\nTherefore, the length of a side of a single container is [MATH: 2\\,\\textrm{ft}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nFive identical cubic dice have the combined volume of [MATH: 40cm^{3}.] Find the side length of a single die.\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3cm]\nb. [MATH: 2cm]\nc. [MATH: root(40, 3)cm]\nd. [MATH: 8cm]\ne. [MATH: 10cm]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\nEach side of an ice cube has length [MATH: (1)/(2)in.] What is the volume of the ice cube?\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(8)in^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 8in^{3}]\nc. [MATH: (1)/(4)in^{3}]\nd. [MATH: (3)/(2)in^{3}]\ne. [MATH: 2in^{3}]"
  }
